## Change

`MQClientInstance.parseOffsetTableFromBroker` mutates each input
`MessageQueue` key's topic and stores that same object in its returned
`HashMap`. If iteration later reaches a null key, Java throws after retaining
earlier key mutations. Rust now borrows `Option<&BrokerOffsetTable>`, where the
shared insertion-ordered input carries nullable shared queue keys and nullable
offsets. It mutates those queue objects in place, returns a new `HashMap` with
the same queue handles, and preserves prefix mutation on error. Null/empty
namespace still copies entries without mutating keys. The existing fixture
already captured both original and mutated input rows, including the
valid-key-before-null LinkedHashMap case; the Rust regression now checks those
mutations and returned-key pointer identity across all seventeen cases.

